the A lines. And so sometimes the

00:30:28.120 --> 00:30:31.520
homologation rewards the most unstable gliders.

00:30:33.000 --> 00:30:36.920
We also have really stable gliders that go well through

00:30:36.920 --> 00:30:39.600
homologation, especially when you use folding lines.

00:30:40.200 --> 00:30:44.720
But as a general tendency, if you have a lot of A's in your

00:30:44.720 --> 00:30:47.680
reports, especially in the collapses, it could mean two

00:30:47.680 --> 00:30:49.640
things. First, that you've done a really

00:30:49.640 --> 00:30:54.240
good job at getting this behaviour right, or that you've

00:30:54.240 --> 00:30:57.320
made the glider so easy to collapse and so unstable that

00:30:57.320 --> 00:31:00.080
just collapses happen all the time and don't do anything.

00:31:01.080 --> 00:31:03.760
But I think in real life this is not what you want.

00:31:03.760 --> 00:31:07.560
When you're flying next to the Cliff in the +6 thermal, then

00:31:07.560 --> 00:31:09.040
you don't want the glider to collapse at all.

00:31:09.880 --> 00:31:15.320
And and so using the report to basically decide on whether or

00:31:15.320 --> 00:31:17.640
not the glider is right for you, I think it's not the right way

00:31:17.640 --> 00:31:20.520
to go. I would put more faith and trust

00:31:20.520 --> 00:31:24.320
on the manufacturer's speech than the mitigation report

00:31:24.360 --> 00:31:28.320
because I think what pilots need is category rather than

00:31:28.440 --> 00:31:32.800
certification on its own. And unless I mean, this is also

00:31:32.800 --> 00:31:35.800
why it's so debated at the moment with competition classes.

